  <title>1.11.4: Identifying the Features of a Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00236</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.11.4: Identifying the Features of a Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8e1676c9-edf5-4863-9ebe-3a242ac39490</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>For numbers 1–3, consider the equation \(1.5x + 4.5y = 18\).</para>
<para>Consider the equation \( 1.5x + 4.5y = 18 \). For each questionexplain your reasoning.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>If we graph the equation, what is the slope of the graph? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The slope is \( -\frac13 \). Rearranging the equation to isolate \( y \) gives \( y=- \frac13 x+4 \).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Where does the graph intersect the \( y \)-axis?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The graph intersects the \( y \)-axis at \( (0,4) \). The 4 in \( y=- \frac13 x+4 \) tells us where the graph crosses the \( y \)-axis. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Where does it intersect the \( x \)-axis?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The graph intersects the \( x \)-axis at \( (12,0) \). Substituting 0 for \( y \) in the original equation and solving for \( x \) gives \( x=12 \).</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para> Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool down in 1.12 and 1.13, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para>
</section>
</content>
